      "text": "The clone file is written to `/var/folders/gb/m6478s0n5mx9wqlz8t_c03800000gn/T/gumshoe-agent-run-k460Sh/clone.html`, and its screenshot is byte-for-byte identical to the original's.\n\n**Summary of decisions:**\n\n- **Source inspection**: The fixture page (`page.html`) ships zero CSS and its only visible content is produced by an inline `<script>` that, after a 150ms `setTimeout`, replaces empty `.placeholder` divs with three `.product-card` elements built from a hardcoded `products` array (name + price), each with an `<img src=\"/product.jpg\">`.\n- **Handling the JS-driven part**: Rather than flagging this as \"unrecreatable,\" I treated it as fully knowable — the data and logic are right there in plain, deterministic JavaScript (no real network fetch, no external API). So I transcribed the exact same script verbatim into the clone, preserving the authentic placeholder → swap behavior rather than just hardcoding the end state.\n- **Componentization**: `.product-card` (image + name + price) is the one clear reusable component in the markup; `.placeholder` is a transient loading stand-in, not worth separate styling.\n- **Theme/base styling**: The original has no stylesheet at all — headings/paragraphs render with plain browser UA defaults (serif `h1`, default margins). Rather than defaulting to a designed look, I loaded Tailwind (as required) but set `corePlugins: { preflight: false }` so Tailwind's reset doesn't strip the native browser defaults that are actually the page's real \"theme.\" No extra utility classes were needed since there's no custom design to encode.\n- **Assets**: `/product.jpg` doesn't exist anywhere (not in the fixture directory, not resolvable from `file://`), so it renders as a broken image with alt text in both the original and the clone — consistent, unmodified behavior, not a fabricated placeholder.\n- **Screenshot comparison**: Captured both pages headlessly via real Chrome (`--headless --window-size=1280,900`) at `original.png` and `clone-round1.png` in the run directory. A byte-level `cmp` showed the two PNGs are identical, so one round was sufficient — no further iteration was needed."
